WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.769
Scenario 6 walkthrough: Structured Data Extraction.

2
00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:14.217
An extraction pipeline that invents values is worse than one that leaves blanks, so this scenario tests schemas, validation loops and when to send work to a human.

3
00:00:15.617 --> 00:00:19.156
Scenario six is a structured data extraction pipeline.

4
00:00:19.156 --> 00:00:29.247
It reads unstructured documents such as invoices or contracts, pulls out fields, validates them against a JSON schema and passes them on to other systems.

5
00:00:29.247 --> 00:00:34.817
The hard parts are edge cases, missing data and knowing how much to trust the output.

6
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:37.569
Questions come from domains four and five.

7
00:00:38.969 --> 00:00:52.166
Here you need structured output through tool use and JSON schemas, few shot examples for documents that look different from each other, validation with targeted retries, and human review with calibrated confidence.

8
00:00:52.166 --> 00:00:57.593
Message Batches also appear, since large extraction backlogs are a natural fit for them.

9
00:00:58.993 --> 00:01:00.120
A common question.

10
00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:05.628
Some invoices have no purchase order number, but the extracted data always contains one.

11
00:01:05.628 --> 00:01:10.761
The schema makes the field required, so the model invents something to satisfy it.

12
00:01:10.761 --> 00:01:14.078
Make the field nullable so a blank is a valid answer.

13
00:01:14.078 --> 00:01:21.777
For categories, use an enum with an other option and a detail field, so unusual cases are not forced into the wrong bucket.

14
00:01:23.177 --> 00:01:24.641
Now the retry decision.

15
00:01:24.641 --> 00:01:30.242
A format error is worth a retry, as long as you send back the specific validation error.

16
00:01:30.242 --> 00:01:36.415
But if the information is simply not in the document, retries will not create it, so return null.

17
00:01:36.415 --> 00:01:41.952
For totals, extract both the stated total and your calculated one, and flag a conflict.

18
00:01:41.952 --> 00:01:45.897
Low confidence and conflicting items go to human review first.

19
00:01:47.297 --> 00:01:48.294
Watch for these.

20
00:01:48.294 --> 00:01:53.278
Believing a schema guarantees correct values, when it only guarantees the shape.

21
00:01:53.278 --> 00:01:55.832
Generic retries without the actual error.

22
00:01:55.832 --> 00:02:02.560
Cutting human review because the overall accuracy looks good, when one document type might be failing badly.

23
00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.607
And trusting confidence scores that were never checked against labelled examples.

24
00:02:09.007 --> 00:02:10.107
For scenario six.

25
00:02:10.107 --> 00:02:15.286
Nullable fields stop the model inventing values, and enums need an escape hatch.

26
00:02:15.286 --> 00:02:21.177
Retry format problems with the specific error, but do not retry when the data is not there.

27
00:02:21.177 --> 00:02:26.226
Look at accuracy per document type and per field before reducing human review.

28
00:02:26.226 --> 00:02:29.657
And only trust confidence scores you have calibrated.

